<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Firth of Tay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Firth of Tay is the estuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tay">River Tay</a> on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a> to the south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a> and the city of Dundee to the north. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it marked a boundary between Pictish territories. It is crossed by the Tay Rail Bridge, whose predecessor collapsed in a storm in 1879 with the loss of all aboard a train, and by the Tay Road Bridge.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/firth-of-tay.jpg" alt="Firth of Tay" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Kelisi at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firthoftaymap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
